// Practice quiz: One-to-many and many-to-one relationships
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/relationships-and-transactions/one-to-many-and-many-to-one-relationships

::o1 mappedBy::What does the value of mappedBy name? {
=The field on the other entity that owns the relationship#Correct. It refers to a Java field.
~The database table#Not quite. It is not a table name.
~The foreign key column#Not quite. @JoinColumn names the column.
~The primary key#Not quite. It is not a key name.
}

::o2 owning side::In a one-to-many and many-to-one pair, which side is the owning side? {
=The many-to-one side, which holds the foreign key#Correct. The row with the column owns it.
~The side with the collection#Not quite. That is the inverse side.
~Whichever is declared first#Not quite. Declaration order does not decide it.
~Both equally#Not quite. One side drives the column.
}

::o2 inverse only change::You only add a child to the parent collection and save. What happens to the foreign key? {
=It stays null, because the owning side was not set#Correct. The inverse side does not write the column.
~It is set from the collection#Not quite. That is the common misconception.
~The save fails#Not quite. It often succeeds silently with null.
~It is set after a restart#Not quite. A restart changes nothing.
}

::o3 sync helper::Why write an addSession helper on the parent? {
=It sets both sides in one place#Correct. Both Java views and the owning side stay right.
~Hibernate requires a method with that name#Not quite. There is no required name.
~It avoids using transactions#Not quite. Transactions are still needed.
~It makes the foreign key unique#Not quite. That is a constraint matter.
}

::o3 stale view::A child sets only its parent field. In the same transaction, what does the parent collection show if already loaded? {
=It may not include the child#Correct. Java objects do not update each other.
~It always includes the child#Not quite. Nothing updates the collection for you.
~It throws an exception#Not quite. It just shows stale data.
~It reloads itself#Not quite. A loaded collection is not refreshed automatically.
}

